== A Word for the Ministers

A word or two for my brethren's sake of the #emph[first doctrine] raised from the foregoing verse, which is the principal point there intended by our Savior, which was this.

1\. That all needful succor, and good entertainment ought to be afforded in special to the ministers of the Gospel.

Our Savior in commending objects to our charity, in the first place mentioneth #emph[prophets,] and the #emph[righteous ones,] saying, #emph[he that receiveth a prophet in the name of a prophet, shall receive a Prophet's reward;] and then addeth, #emph[he that receiveth a righteous man in the name of a righteous man, etc.]

Indeed, so general must be the extent and emanation of our charity, that it must not exclude any who are in want and distress. For though we may, and ought to put a difference between men upon whom we bestow our charity; yet ought we not to restrain it from any men who are in want; but must act the #emph[grace of charity] towards them who have not the #emph[grace of faith.] Yea, #emph[we ought to do good unto all,] as the #emph[Apostle] exhorteth, #emph[Gal 6:13. But especially unto them who are of the household of faith.] And among them chiefly to the #emph[ministers of Christ.] So as this object of charity, #emph[prophet,] is not here to be taken #emph[exclusively,] but by way of #emph[eminency] and #emph[preferment:] ministers are #emph[before,] and #emph[above others,] with more #emph[readiness] and #emph[cheerfulness] to be ministered unto.

The people of #emph[Israel] we read were commanded once and again not to leave nor forsake their #emph[Levites,] especially when they were reduced into any straits and exigencies, as Deut 12:19. And 14. 27. Then much less should the #emph[ministers of the Gospel] be left and forsaken by the people of God, when they lye under any want or distress.

The examples of God's people that have gone before us in the performance of this duty are many, both in the old and new Testament. It is recorded to the perpetual praise and commendation of #emph[Obadiah,] that he hid the #emph[prophets of God] by fifty in a cave from the cruelty of #emph[Jezebel,] and sustained them with food, and ministered all things necessary unto them, 1 Kgs 18:13. We read likewise how the widow of #emph[Sarephath] nourished the prophet #emph[Elijah] in her house for many days and years, even so long as the famine continued, 1 Kgs 17:15, 16. The kindness of the good #emph[Shunamite] to the prophet #emph[Elisha,] is largely reproved, 2 Kgs 4:8, etc. #emph[It fell on a day that] Elisha #emph[passed to] Shunem, #emph[where was a great woman, and she constrained him to eat bread;] that is, to refresh himself with food. #emph[And so it was, that as oft as he passed by, he turned in thither to eat bread.] Having found good entertainment, and hearty welcome, he did the oftener visit the family. #emph[And she said unto her husband, behold now I perceive, that this is an holy man of God, which passeth by us continually, let us make a little chamber, I pray thee, on the wall, and let us set for him there a bed, and a table, and a stool, and a candlestick.] Under these, other necessary accommodations are comprised. The good woman desired of her husband, #emph[that a chamber might be made for the prophet on the wall;] perhaps not that she wanted room in her house to lodge him in, but because she considered, that the noise of a great family might be some disturbance to the Prophet's study and devotion; therefore thought it convenient to provide such a chamber for him, where he might be more retired, and have the free ingress and egress; that by those accommodations he might be encouraged the oftener to take up his lodging there.

Rom 16:2. It is set down as the height and crown of #emph[Phebe]'s good works, that she had been #emph[a succorer of the Apostle Paul in his wants and straits.] And in 2 Timothy 1:16. We read how #emph[Onesiphorus] refreshed and relieved the #emph[Apostle] in his suffering condition, and #emph[was not ashamed of his chain;] that is, though he were a prisoner, and under restraint for the gospels sake, yet #emph[Onesiphorus] owned him, showed him all respect, succored and relieved him in that his low condition. #emph[Gaius] likewise, a godly, wealthy citizen of #emph[Corinth,] is famous for that kind entertainment and succor which he gave to the #emph[Apostle,] and the church with him, Rom 16:23.

It is recorded to the commendation of King #emph[Edward] the sixth, that he afforded all needful succor, and good entertainment to #emph[Bucer, Fagius,] and other divines, when they fled into this kingdom by reason of the troubles in their own nation. And #emph[Beza] in his epistle #emph[ad Regem Jacobum,] maketh an honorable mention of a liberal collection of the church of #emph[Scotland] sent to the #emph[French-ministers,] who were banished #emph[Religionis causa.]

Here are fair copies for us to write after. And indeed why are these things written, and left upon record to posterity, but for our learning? Not so much for our #emph[admiration,] as for our #emph[imitation,] that we might follow their steps, and write after their copy; which #emph[St.] Paul #emph[testifies of the] Macedonians, 2 Cor 9:2. How that very many of them were stirred up and provoked to a liberal contribution to the poor saints at #emph[Jerusalem] by the forwardness and zeal of the #emph[Corinthians. And St.] Austin (Confes. Lib. 9. cap. 2.) #emph[confesseth, that] the examples of God's servants, did burn and consume his own lukewarmness; and their fervent zeal set an edge on his devotion.

To these examples I shall add a few reasons for the better enforcing this duty upon you.

Reas. I. #emph[Because ministers being supported, are like to be most serviceable to the church of God.] They are #emph[spiritual fathers,] to beget men unto Christ. They are his #emph[Ambassadors,] and stand in his stead to reveal the mysteries of the kingdom, sent forth to be reconcilers betwixt God and this sinful world; to pray and beseech them to be reconciled unto God, 2 Cor 5:20. Their work is to bear the name of God, to publish the salvation of God before the world; to open the eyes of the blind, and to turn them from darkness to light, and from the power of Satan unto God, that they may receive forgiveness of sins, and an inheritance among them that are sanctified: in which respect #emph[ministers of the Gospel] are more useful and serviceable in their generation, than either #emph[Lawyers, physicians] or #emph[tradesmen,] which yet are useful enough.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. #emph[Lawyers] indeed may be a means of settling your right in your goods and lands: but #emph[ministers] of settling your right to your heavenly inheritance.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. #emph[Physicians] may cure the maladies of your bodies: but #emph[ministers] are sent to cure the diseases of your souls.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. #emph[Merchants,] and all sorts of tradesmen may replenish a land with all manner of commodities necessary for this life: but #emph[ministers] only bring in such heavenly and spiritual treasures as spring up into the everlasting treasure.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[So that by supplying their bodily wants, you do comfort and refresh the souls of many. And what better charity imaginable, than by relieving the temporal wants of some few, to further the everlasting salvation of many? Oh what cause will their souls have to bless God to all eternity for your charity, who have been converted by the ministry of those whom you have refreshed and supported in their work!]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Reas. II. #emph[another Reason may be taken from the consideration of the quality of the persons for whom I am pleading:] they are not so mean as outwardly they appear. They whom you behold so poorly clad, walking so djectedly, being destitute of many outward comforts, are men endowed with many singular prerogatives and privileges, not only above the rest of God's people; but likewise above the most #emph[glorious angels.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Compare them first with the people of God; they are called the #emph[Lord's sheep;] these #emph[the shepherds of the flock,] Ezek 34:2. They #emph[children] in God's family; these #emph[spiritual fathers,] who by the seed of the word beget men unto God, 1 Cor 4:15. Therefore the Spirit of God vouchsafeth them this dignity, to be called συνεργοὶ τοῦ Θεοῦ, #emph[fellow-laborers with God.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Concerning #emph[angels;] true it is that they and ministers are #emph[fellow-servants,] Rev 22:9. But yet ministers have sundry prerogatives above them.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. #emph[Angels rejoice at the conversion of a sinner,] Luke 15:10. But by the Ministery of #emph[prophets] sinners are converted unto God, #emph[act.] 26. 18.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. An angel was sent to #emph[Cornelius,] to advise him whither to go to be instructed: but a minister instructed him in the way of life, #emph[act.] 10. 6. 34.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. Was the word of reconciliation ever committed to #emph[angels?] To ministers it is committed, 2 Cor 5:18. It is observable, that the Embassage of ministers is not simply ἀγγελία, #emph[Message,] but εὐαγγέλιον, #emph[the Gospel,] the gladsome tidings of salvation by Jesus Christ, #emph[into which the angels themselves do desire to look and pry,] as the Apostle #emph[Peter] expresseth, 1 Pet 1:12. The Lord hath also committed to his #emph[ministers] the administration of the sacraments, which are the seals of his covenant, whereby all his precious promises are ratified, and confirmed unto us. So that as in respect of the rich treasures of God's word committed to their dispensing, they are the #emph[Lord's treasurers:] so in respect of the sacraments, they are the #emph[Keepers of his great seal.] If these officers be great under mortal princes, what are they under the King of Kings?]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[I have taken the liberty in a few words to set forth some of the privileges of the ministers of the Gospel; partly for the comfort and consolation of many of them under the present misery and poverty which they suffer, rather than to sin against their consciences: and partly for the incitation of others to afford them that #emph[double honor,] which the Apostle calls for, 1 Tim 5:17. Namely, #emph[the honor of respect,] and the #emph[honor of maintenance,] that they suffer them not to sink under their burthen, but afford them some seasonable succor and relief, suitable to their present wants and necessities.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Reas. III. #emph[Because ministers are oftenest exposed to sufferings and distresses, and so have most need of relief.] The bow of the adversaries of religion is bent most against the teachers and leaders of the people. That word, #emph[1 Kgs 22:31. Fight neither with small or great, save only with the King of] Israel, is often changed into, #emph[Fight neither with small or great, but only against the prophets of the Lord.] Smite the #emph[Shepherds,] and the #emph[sheep] will be scattered. Experience enough tells the world who they are that are set in the front of the battle, and who are the first that fall by the power of the adversaries. And #emph[who should be most in the eye of charity, but those that are most in the eye of malice?]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Besides, for whose sakes is it that ministers are so often brought so low? Is it not for your sakes, whose souls are dearer to them than their own substance? Is it not often upon this account, because they will be faithful to you, and will not keep silence, nor cease to watch over you, and to warn, and to instruct you from day today?]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Might they not save themselves more whole, if they would hold their peace, and let the #emph[devil] alone to have his will of you? For your sakes no doubt it is, that much of their sufferings come upon them; and shall they be forsaken by you? The truth is, it is upon this account a piece of #emph[justice,] as well as a piece of #emph[charity,] to relieve and succor them; and so you may do two good works in one; the same alms may be both a work of righteousness, and a work of mercy.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Reas. IV. #emph[Your gratitude to God requireth a cheerful performance of this duty from you.] For wherein can you otherwise make a suitable return to God, for what he hath plentifully conferred on you, than by contributing some of your #emph[Temporals] to them, from whom ye reap so much in #emph[Spirituals?] The Lord of his free grace and rich mercy hath abounded to you in these outward things; #emph[he hath given you richly all things to enjoy.] Yea, as the #emph[psalmist] speaketh, #emph[he hath loaded you with his benefits.] Now, not to return some part of your estate to him, who hath given you all that you do enjoy, were ingratitude in the highest degree: and therefore how doth it concern you often to put the #emph[psalmist]'s question to yourselves, #emph[what shall I render unto the Lord for all his benefits towards me?] Ps 116:12. Surely one kindness deserveth another; as God hath been bountiful to you, sure you are very unworthy, if you be not bountiful to his. And how do you think he will take it at your hands? Will he ever say to you, #emph[Well done good and faithful servant, thou hast been faithful, a good steward of my many gifts?] Or, is it all one to you whether he say, #emph[Well done, or not?]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And in regard you cannot directly and immediately requite him who is incapable of being enriched by us; #emph[my goodness extendeth not to thee,] psalm 16. 2. It will be your wisdom to express your thankfulness to God by being kind to his poor relations. Now, who are nearer to God than his ministers? Whom he hath appointed to stand in his room, to declare his mind unto his people; who are as the mouth of the people unto God; so God's mouth unto the people; who are the messengers of the Lord of hosts, by whose ministry there is peace concluded, and reconciliation proclaimed between God and man. Surely what kindness you show to these, he takes as done unto himself, Matt 25:40.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Reas. V. #emph[Your relieving the faithful ministers of Christ, may in a sense be said to have something more in it, than if you should show your charity to himself, in his own person, as being a sign of greater love.] It is but an ordinary thing to return kindness to our dearest friends; but to extend our good will to their relations, or servants for their sakes, is an evidence of much greater love. For if upon their account only we do good unto these; how much more would it be judged we should be ready to do it unto themselves, if they stood in need of our help? And so will Christ look upon it: our bounty expressed to his ministers he will receive as an evidence of our more abundant love unto him, which he will in no wise forget, nor suffer to go unrewarded.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Reas. VI. #emph[Such as relieve the poor ministers of Christ, shall have their blessing, the benefit of their prayers unto God.] They will bless you however, though you have no blessing for them. Though you have not an alms for them, yet they will have a prayer for you. But their bowels being refreshed by you, you may expect that their heart will be the more enlarged for you; and this will be a far greater kindness than you can bestow on them. Your gift cannot be worth their fervent prayers. In this respect it is much better to give, than to receive; because he that receives hath but a perishing alms; he that gives, receives an abiding blessing, the blessing of prayers.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Herein did the Apostle St. #emph[Paul,] make a large requital to #emph[Onesiphorus] for the kindness he showed to him in his imprisonment; as you may read, #emph[2 Tim 1:16. The Lord give mercy unto the house of] Onesiphorus, #emph[for he oft refreshed me, and was not ashamed of my chain.] As if he had said, I received much comfort and refreshment from #emph[Onesiphorus,] when I was in prison, for the which I beseech the Father of all mercies, to show mercy unto him, and his, by blessing him, and his whole family, with all blessings, both temporal, spiritual and eternal. And indeed what blessing can that man want, who hath a share in many ministers prayers? Which are available for the obtaining of all good things at the hand of God, as for themselves, so for others. Yea the very work of charity doth bespeak a blessing from God. As the blood of #emph[Abel] is said to cry to the Lord for a curse or vengeance upon #emph[Cain:] so every act of charity crieth to God for a blessing upon the charitable. Our very acts of charity in relieving the wants of the poor, and refreshing their bowels, do pray and cry to God for his blessing upon us and ours.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Reas. VI. #emph[Your charity to them will be hereafter rewarded with everlasting glory and happiness in the kingdom of heaven.] This reason our Savior himself giveth in these words; #emph[he that receiveth a prophet in the name of a prophet, shall receive a prophets reward;] whereby is meant that surpassing and excellent weight of glory, which cannot be expressed by the tongue of men or angels. Yea, by the reward of a #emph[prophet] seemeth to be implied the highest degree of heavenly glory. For Dan 12:3. It is said, #emph[that they that be wise,] that is, the righteous, who are endowed with true spiritual wisdom, being lightened by the Spirit of God, #emph[shall shine as the brightness of the firmament; but they that turn many to righteousness,] which is the work of #emph[prophets, shall shine as the stars for ever and ever.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Now by your bounty to the #emph[prophets of God,] you may come to partake of their reward. So that it is your own concernment to contribute liberally towards their relief. Not that your charity to them doth merit that glorious reward; but in regard of the promise of God made to all those, who do exercise the same towards his ministers, it shall be truly given unto them.]]
